ide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. The changed lines of the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) are checked against two per-worker-role thresholds. Over `diff_max_lines` (default 3,000) also holds, as `diff guard: N changed lines, limit M`. Over `diff_warn_lines` (default 2,000) but not over the limit, the PR goes ahead: the loop logs `worker.diff_large` (lines, warn, max), and the PR body and the close-out issue comment carry a fixed note, `Large change: N changed lines (warn at W)`, so reviewers know; the note quotes nothing from the change. The latest step's count decides, so a fix round that shrinks the PR drops the note. The comment shows a rejection reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n 6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n 7. **Test.** Run `tests` (default `make test`; empty disables the step) as `/bin/sh -c` in the role's image, with no credential. On failure the agent gets the failure output in a fix step, which commits and pushes again, up to `max_test_fixes` (default 2) within the same job. A fix that changes no files, the cap, or the time running out ends the loop with the tests failed. Test logs stay on the host (kept 7 days) and are never committed.\n-8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the candidates are the role's `reviewers` (a list that only narrows), or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty, and each candidate's `when:` still decides. A `when:` matches when any path changed between the merge base of the config commit and the pushed head matches a `paths` entry, or a listed triage role ran (closed `done`, or its `ard:triage:<role>:done` label; `needs-human` does not count). Paths are case-sensitive and anchored at the repo root: `web/` is a directory, anything else a doublestar glob, so `**/go.mod` matches a root `go.mod` and `go.mod` only the root one; `.ard/` and `.ard-input/` paths are never selectable. Any `review:<role>` the lead deferred or the worker's own `next` lists is added regardless of `when:`, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. Selection is monotonic: each run recomputes from the whole PR diff and close-out only adds labels, so earlier requests stay and newly matching reviewers are added. When the head or git is unavailable, path rules fail open. If no reviewer is selected the PR stays a draft (the loop logs `worker.no_reviewers`). The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a failure, so pushed work is never left without one.\n+8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the candidates are the role's `reviewers` (a list that only narrows), or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty, and each candidate's `when:` still decides. A `when:` matches when any path changed between the merge base of the config commit and the pushed head matches a `paths` entry, or a listed triage role ran (closed `done`, or its `ard:triage:<role>:done` label; `needs-human` does not count). Paths are case-sensitive and anchored at the repo root: `web/` is a directory, anything else a doublestar glob, so `**/go.mod` matches a root `go.mod` and `go.mod` only the root one; `.ard/` and `.ard-input/` paths are never selectable. Any `review:<role>` the lead deferred or the worker's own `next` lists is added regardless of `when:`, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. Selection is monotonic: each run recomputes from the whole PR diff and close-out only adds labels, so earlier requests stay and newly matching reviewers are added. When the head or git is unavailable, path rules fail open. If no reviewer is selected the PR stays a draft (the loop logs `worker.no_reviewers`). The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a failure, so pushed work is never left without one. **PR creation failure and recovery (#435).** `CreatePR` is retried when the failure is transient (`github.IsTransient`: 5xx, rate limit, network timeout), up to 4 attempts with 2 s, 5 s and 15 s waits; before each retry the loop looks for a PR on the head, in case the failed create reached GitHub. Under dry-run it logs `worker.pr_retry` and does not retry. When the create still fails after this job pushed the branch, the job records the branch, head, PR title and body, summary and worker result in the nullable `jobs.pending_pr` column (migration 0009; older rows read as none) and ends `executor:failed` with the cause `pr_create_failed` (not transient, not auto-retried) and one comment naming the branch and short head: only the PR is missing. When a human removes `executor:failed`, the next worker job looks at the newest earlier worker job of the issue and role; if it has a record, the branch tip is its recorded head (or only adds files under `.ard/`, which is the failed job's own close-out commit) and no open PR exists for the branch, the loop opens the PR from the record and closes the job done with no agent run and no test run (`WorkerResult.Reopened`; close-out reads no role file and takes the recorded summary). Otherwise, or if the lookup fails, the full worker runs as before. The built-in engineer prompt also tells the agent to answer `done` with the original summary when the branch already holds the complete change and no PR exists.\n 9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR. A role file that says `needs-network` on a first job (no branch, no PR, nothing pushed) bounces the issue to the triage engineer once, as described under the role-file contract; in any other case it ends `needs-human`, nothing is pushed, and the comment lists the needed inputs.\n \n **The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it fetches nothing and answers `needs-network` with a `network:` list (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`) when no branch exists yet, or `needs-human` when a branch or PR does (#301, #384). The controller bounces a first `needs-network` once; the second ends `needs-human`.\ndiff --git a/internal/closeout/closeout.go b/internal/closeout/closeout.go\nindex 02abc32..4382a89 100644\n--- a/internal/closeout/closeout.go\n+++ b/internal/closeout/closeout.go\n@@ -118,6 +118,11 @@ const (\n \t// not a failure and never hands off to the automatic retry (Retry is\n \t// false); the issue is re-claimed after the reset.\n \tCauseUsageLimit CauseKind = \"usage_limit\"\n+\t// CausePRCreateFailed: the worker pushed its branch but GitHub would not\n+\t// open the pull request, even after retries. Not transient and never\n+\t// retried automatically: the job ends executor:failed, and the loop opens\n+\t// the PR from the recorded close-out when the label is removed.\n+\tCausePRCreateFailed CauseKind = \"pr_create_failed\"\n )\n \n // Cause is why a job ended. Detail is fixed, non-secret text.\ndiff --git a/internal/closeout/prepare.go b/internal/closeout/prepare.go\nindex 23b1fa5..dd8dc86 100644\n--- a/internal/closeout/prepare.go\n+++ b/internal/closeout/prepare.go\n@@ -79,6 +79,9 @@ func Prepare(ctx context.Context, j Job, d Deps) (Record, error) {\n \t\tif j.Cause.Detail != \"\" {\n \t\t\tfailed += \": \" + j.Cause.Detail\n \t\t}\n+\tcase j.Stage == StageWorker && j.Worker != nil && j.Worker.Reopened:\n+\t\t// No agent ran: the result is the earlier job's, passed in Summary.\n+\t\trf = RoleFile{Role: j.Role, Status: StatusDone, Summary: j.Worker.Summary}\n \tcase j.ExitCode != 0:\n \t\tfailed = fmt.Sprintf(\"agent exited %d\", j.ExitCode)\n \tdefault:\n@@ -287,6 +290,13 @@ func Prepare(ctx context.Context, j Job, d Deps) (Record, error) {\n \t\t\tplan = needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, \"\")\n \t\t}\n \t\tplan.Summary = summary\n+\t\tif j.Stage == StageWorker && j.Worker != nil && j.Worker.Reopened {\n+\t\t\tif res, terr := red.Text(ctx, 